.TH READ 2
.SH NAME
read, write, pread, pwrite, read_full, write_full \- read or write a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "read :: proc(fd: int, buf: []u8) -> i64"
.B "write :: proc(fd: int, data: []u8) -> i64"
.B "pread :: proc(fd: int, buf: []u8, offset: u64) -> i64"
.B "pwrite :: proc(fd: int, data: []u8, offset: u64) -> i64"
.B "read_full :: proc(fd: int, buf: []u8) -> bool"
.B "write_full :: proc(fd: int, data: []u8) -> bool"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Read
reads up to
.B len(buf)
bytes from the descriptor's current offset into
.IR buf ,
and answers how many bytes it read; the offset moves by that many.
It is not guaranteed that the buffer fills: a read of the console answers
one line, and a read of a pipe answers what is already there.
Zero is end of file.
.PP
.I Write
writes
.B len(data)
bytes from
.I data
at the descriptor's current offset, and answers how many bytes landed; the
offset moves by that many.
A short write, fewer bytes than
.IR data 's
length, should be treated as an error.
.PP
.I Pread
and
.I pwrite
read or write at
.I offset
without reading the descriptor's own cursor and without moving it
afterward, so two threads sharing a descriptor may transfer at different
offsets without disturbing each other.
.PP
.I Read_full
and
.I write_full
loop
.I read
or
.I write
until the whole buffer has moved.
One call may move less than asked: a kernel copy is bounded to a few
kilobytes a pass, and a stream may answer short at any point.
Each answers
.B false
if the descriptor ended \(em end of file, or an error \(em before the
whole buffer crossed.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/sys.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/syscall.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2),
.IR dup (2),
.IR open (2),
.IR pipe (2),
.IR seek (2),
.IR stat (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Each call answers a non-negative count on success and a negative
.B \-errno
on failure; see
.IR errstr (2).
.B EBADF
is a descriptor that is not open for the direction asked.
.SH BUGS
A read or write cut short by a note may have moved an unknown number of
bytes, as Plan 9's does.
